Open-Source Community Grows

=========

  Step 1: Harrison wrote the code on his own computer

Initially, LangChain was just a normal Python project on Harrison Chase's laptop.

Example:

langchain/
│
├── llm.py
├── prompt.py
├── chains.py
├── memory.py
├── tools.py
├── __init__.py
└── setup.py

At this stage, nobody else could see it.

Step 2: Test the project locally

He tested it on his computer.

Example:

python demo.py

If it worked, he improved it.

Developers usually repeat this cycle many times:

Write code
      ↓
Run
      ↓
Bug
      ↓
Fix
      ↓
Run again
Step 3: Create a Git repository

Before publishing, developers typically use Git, a version control system.

git init

Git tracks every change made to the project.

Step 4: Create a GitHub account

Harrison already had a GitHub account called hwchase17. The earliest version of LangChain was published from his personal GitHub account before later moving to the LangChain organisation.

GitHub is simply a website for hosting Git repositories.

Laptop
   │
Git
   │
GitHub
Step 5: Create a new GitHub repository

On GitHub he clicked:

New Repository

Repository name:

langchain

Initially it looked something like:

langchain/

README.md

LICENSE

.gitignore
Step 6: Upload the code

From his computer he connected the local Git repository to GitHub.

Typical commands are:

git add .
git commit -m "Initial version"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/hwchase17/langchain.git
git push -u origin main

Now the code was available online.

Step 7: Choose an Open-Source License

Uploading code alone does not automatically make it open source.

The author must also choose a licence.

LangChain uses the MIT License, which is a very permissive open-source licence.

Typical repository:

langchain/

README.md
LICENSE
setup.py
langchain/

The licence tells everyone what they are allowed to do with the code.

Step 8: Write Documentation

He created a README.md file.

This explains:

What LangChain is
Installation
Example code
Features
How to contribute

Example:

# LangChain

pip install langchain

from langchain import OpenAI

Without good documentation, people rarely adopt an open-source project.

Step 9: Publish to PyPI

GitHub stores the source code.

To let Python users install it with one command, Harrison also published LangChain to PyPI (Python Package Index).

Users could then install it by running:

pip install langchain

PyPI is to Python what the Play Store is to Android apps.

Step 10: Announce the Project

After publishing, Harrison announced LangChain on social media and to developer communities. His first public announcement described it as "a Python package aimed at helping build LLM applications through composability."

Developers started trying it.

Step 11: Accept Community Contributions

Once people found bugs or wanted new features, they contributed using GitHub.

Typical workflow:

Developer
      │
Fork Repository
      │
Write Code
      │
Commit
      │
Create Pull Request
      │
Harrison Reviews
      │
Merge

This is how open-source software grows.

Step 12: Build a Community

As LangChain became popular, it attracted:

Thousands of GitHub stars
Hundreds of contributors
Documentation writers
Bug reporters
Feature requests

It eventually moved from Harrison's personal repository to the langchain-ai GitHub organisation as the project and company grew.

